================ DIAGNOSIS ================
[randomness: minor] one sample r_sy=0.617, two samples r_ss=0.790, model mean r_inf=0.694: removing mouth randomness is worth +0.077. -> the gap is mostly not sampling noise.
[bias] samples agree with each other (r_ss=0.790) more than with the real lips (r_sy=0.617): a consistent error, i.e. bias -> audio features, data, supervision.
[ceiling for this model] even its mean prediction reaches only 0.694; 1 - r_inf = 0.306 is bias plus what audio cannot determine (see probe).
[timing] 17% of clips peak at |lag| >= 2 frames; allowing the best lag per clip raises corr 0.678 -> 0.774 (+0.096). Per-clip timing errors are a real share of the gap.
[timing precision: ok] dropping the audio raises the model's mouth loss +11.3%; shifting it 1 / 2 frames raises it +2.6% / +8.9%, i.e. an 80 ms error costs 79% of losing the audio.
[data offsets (model's view): COMMON] the model fits 16% of clips best with the audio shifted >= 2 frames (median shift +0.0).
[data offsets (SyncNet on real clips): COMMON] 41% of real clips are off by >= 2 frames, 9% have confidence < 3. Lip corr on aligned clips 0.659 vs offset clips 0.410.
  -> correct per-clip offsets and filter low-confidence clips (report section 3a); offset VAL clips also depress the measured corr itself.
[reference shortcut: no] an open- vs closed-mouth reference moves the generated mean opening by +0.30 real-std; corr 0.631 (closed ref) / 0.634 (frame 0) / 0.641 (open ref).
[language gap: YES] lip corr English 0.645 (n=261) vs other 0.454 (n=37): +0.192. -> multilingual encoder + language embedding (report 5a).
